+      "source_hypotheses": "L > 0, a compact birth interval B, and the centered variance-one Gaussian field on X = R²/(LZ²) with covariance K_L (12-16); any orthonormal frame (u, v), physical k = 1, and observations (1): M = −ru/2, S = ru/2, f(M) = b, f(S) = b − r³, zero gradients, b ∈ B (18-21); Q_r is their canonical Gaussian regression law; F_j(H) = |det H| on nonsingular Hessians with j negative eigenvalues; W_r = F_2(H_M)F_1(H_S), Z_r = E_{Q_r}W_r, dQ_r^W = (W_r/Z_r)dQ_r with the full normalizer (23-29); N counts all additional critical points of every Morse index with height in I_r = (b − r³, b) excluding M, S; N_R those with dist_X(x, 0) < Rr; N_far^ρ those in D_ρ = {dist_X(x, 0) ≥ ρ} (30-32); r small enough that the local cylinder embeds and Rr < ρ/2 (33). The theorem: each fixed R ≥ 4 and integer p ≥ 0, uniform in b ∈ B and frames, 0 < r ≤ r_{R,p}, ρ = r^{1/100} (37-39), with N^0 = 1 (48); conditional on the retained interfaces of §2 (36, 54-91). §4 uses ρ₀ ≤ min(1, L/32), 0 < ρ ≤ ρ₀, r ≤ ρ/8 (151); §6 imposes the cutoffs ρ = r^{1/100}, T = r^{−1/100}, m = 600 and the listed small-r thresholds (241-246). The source also retains DL's planar regional hypotheses (65) and C6's pinned genericity interface (76); its planar exclusion assumes R ≥ 1, rK ≤ 1/(4R) and λ = −A > (8RK + 56RK²)r (110-112), and §§3 and 5 use T ≥ 1 with rT ≤ 1/(4R) (135, 225).",
