+      "classification_reason": "Most of the file writes out existing CAP/P/E2 arguments. PERSISTENCE_BRIDGE.md:3-7 says it 'expands existing CAP/P/E2 arguments'. BRIDGE_SOURCES.json:3 says it 'writes out the existing planar cap/global-selector/marked-pair bridge through its exact determinant-weighted loss inequality', which is (1) at PERSISTENCE_BRIDGE.md:52-58 ('Below we prove the following interface'). The file also adds mathematics of its own. §5 (PERSISTENCE_BRIDGE.md:404-493) rescales the C39 example onto the side-24 torus at arbitrarily small r, which gives (15), and gives a full C^2 support argument for (16). BRIDGE_SOURCES.json:179-196 lists only these two as companion deductions, C46_SIDE24_COMPLETION and C46_FIXED_PIN_SUPPORT, 'not attributed to C39'. The C39 base example is Q-2001, which lies outside this object's file list. The written-out cap statement and interface (1) are kept in this record because the bridge states and argues them in its own words (§§2-4), citing CAP, P and E2 as their sources.",
+      "source_statements": [
+        {
+          "file": "docs/research-translation/20260930/PERSISTENCE_BRIDGE.md",
+          "line_start": 52,
+          "line_end": 58,
+          "text": "There is no pin density or coordinate Jacobian in `Z`. Below we prove the\nfollowing interface, for each fixed positive separation and distinct heights:\n\n```text\nG_cap ⊂ {e=1}                         Q-almost surely,\n0 ≤ Z(1−p) = E_Q[W(1−e)] ≤ E_Q[W 1{G_cap fails}].      (1)\n```"
+        },
+        {
+          "file": "docs/research-translation/20260930/PERSISTENCE_BRIDGE.md",
+          "line_start": 83,
+          "line_end": 85,
+          "text": "The pins have the stated maximum and saddle types. For every global Morse\nextension with distinct critical values, S is M's actual elder death.\nThe proof uses no information about the exterior of D."
+        },
+        {
+          "file": "docs/research-translation/20260930/PERSISTENCE_BRIDGE.md",
+          "line_start": 413,
+          "line_end": 416,
+          "text": "It is not a count of already selected persistence competitors. For this\ncount the inclusion `{e=0}⊂{N_r≥2}` is false. The C39 construction proves\nthis for an exactly enumerated smooth Morse torus function, rather than\nan abstract random integer."
+        },
+        {
+          "file": "docs/research-translation/20260930/PERSISTENCE_BRIDGE.md",
+          "line_start": 445,
+          "line_end": 449,
+          "text": "are preserved. Hence\n\n```text\nd_Fr(M)=b+kr³ε²m > b−kr³,        N_r=1,        (N_r)₂=0. (15)\n```"
+        },
+        {
+          "file": "docs/research-translation/20260930/PERSISTENCE_BRIDGE.md",
+          "line_start": 475,
+          "line_end": 479,
+          "text": "by a positive constant. Therefore, for each fixed such pinned law,\n\n```text\nQ^W{e=0 and N_r=1} > 0.                              (16)\n```"
+        }
+      ],
+      "source_hypotheses": "The source fixes the flat torus R^2/(24Z^2) with the centered unit-variance Gaussian field of normalized periodized covariance and ordinary superlevel H0 (PERSISTENCE_BRIDGE.md:24-37). The roles are ordered: b=f(M), s=b-kr^3 with k>0, a fixed positive separation and distinct heights (:39-53). The cap statement assumes that f is C^4 near D=[-2r,2r]^2 with critical pins, that lambda > [4/(3k)] r M_3^2 and r M_4 <= 3k/10, and that D embeds (a sufficient condition is r < 24/(4 sqrt 2)) (:66-77). Section 5 takes 0 < epsilon < 1/16 and each fixed small r, b, k (:418, :455-456).",
