+      "layer_a_status": "A7 and A8 block closure. No kernel declaration exists, and the enclosure's only support is unverified Python decimal-interval and Taylor-model software, whose soundness assumes correct rounding of the decimal module's exp, ln and sqrt and the Gauss–Legendre error formula. A3: floats occur only in mesh, geometry and order choices (see floating_point); no float output counts as certification. A4: the item neither loosens nor tightens the printed enclosure. A6: no vacuous conclusion.",
+      "exact_boundary": "The recorded stdout of certificate v1's assembly (results/assembly_stdout.txt lines 5-10, byte-identical in assembly_stdout_O.txt) states D in [0.0757052049785748938015641071813, 0.0778600952308480155728023482430], q = J/30 − Ĩ_quad − D in [0.672471569882485317760530985090, 0.674626562011425106198435892820], and the four (c3 − R_{2/3})(L) intervals for d = 2, 3 at L ≥ 10 and L ≥ 24, with the pieces at lines 3-4. dd_exact.py assembles them from the 2393 cells of cells_full.json and the 38,288 node values in nodes_0.json … nodes_11.json. In certificate v1 the node values omit the sliver gaps at 2,694 narrow-regime nodes, so the source does not treat these assembled ends alone as an enclosure of D. By its reading, the true D lies in [D_lo + Δ_lo, D_hi + Δ_hi] with control C10's Δ (dd_gapcheck.py lines 11-12; item v1-c10-gap). Certificate v2's assembled lower end …071809 lies below v1's …071813 (DD/PROOF.md lines 211-212). The source states that this corrected interval lies inside the printed enclosure, with margins 1.56·10⁻²¹ and 2.71·10⁻²⁰, and that v1's printed numbers stand (DD/PROOF.md lines 5, 208-213; README line 27). The printed outward roundings are Theorem DD's [0.0757052049785748938, 0.0778600952308480156] and Corollary DD1. They are the claim that proposed record QP-C54-021 holds at claim level. No literal Lean kernel declaration states D, the enclosure or the corollary at this cut; the Python decimal-interval programs and the stored node values are not admitted as closure. Everything stated about D uses I = Φ and is at #244 Theorem A's conditional scope. The corollary also rests on note CJ's J, note DC's −Ĩ_quad and note V24's η allowances, entered as decimal literals (dd_exact.py lines 17-18, 24-25). No vacuity is indicated.",
+      "blocking_reason": "Missing: (i) kernel formalizations of #244's closed form Φ, of Theorem A's identification I = Φ, of the reduction (1.1)-(1.3) and of Lemmas 1-6, giving D = D_S + D_G ± (ω-tails) ± (τ-tail); (ii) a kernel-checked interval evaluation (or a checker whose own soundness is established) of the 38,288 node values ρH(node) by rigorous v-quadrature, of the 2393 Gauss–Legendre cell remainders, of region S and of the ω- and τ-tails; (iii) for certificate v1, a kernel-checked bound of the omitted sliver-gap contribution; (iv) for Corollary DD1, kernel-checked versions of note CJ's J enclosure, note DC's −Ĩ_quad enclosure and note V24's Theorem V allowances.",
+      "attempted": "Stored programs ddcore.py, ddbounds.py, ddtaylor.py, ddint.py, ddvq.py, ddgauss.py, ddS.py and dd_exact.py; the stored cells and the twelve node files of hosted run 38347192184; the recorded assembly stdout in both interpreter modes. Per README lines 35-36, Slice A read §§0–2 with ddcore.py, ddbounds.py and ddtaylor.py (PASS). Slice B read §§3–5 with ddint.py, ddvq.py, ddS.py, ddgauss.py and dd_exact.py. It recomputed every cell remainder and four nodes and reproduced the assembly (AMEND of eight display roundings, applied in v1.1). The automated OpenAI Codex review of Math-#448 reported the sliver-cell defect (README line 27). All reads have organizational independence 0 (README line 24).",
+      "resumption_cost": "Very large. It needs a kernel formalization of #244's closed form, Theorem A's scope and the six lemmas. It also needs a kernel-grade interval-quadrature engine able to replay about 38,000 rigorous one-dimensional v-quadratures in Taylor-model arithmetic (the source's v1 run took 39–66 minutes per shard on 12 shards; DD/PROOF.md lines 241-242).",
