+      "source_hypotheses": "D is note V24's model number as defined at DD/PROOF.md lines 14-19, with Φ the closed form of #244 (2.5)-(2.7). Every statement about D uses I = Φ and is at #244 Theorem A's conditional scope (#170 Theorem E(1), [CUB] Theorem C and [CUB]'s height identities (C8)-(C11) with its B = 0 paragraph; DD/PROOF.md lines 8-9, 261-263). The assembly takes as given the decomposition D = D_S + D_G ± (ω-tails) ± (τ-tail) (dd_exact.py lines 2-4; DD/PROOF.md line 182). The source derives it from the reduction (1.1)-(1.3) and Lemmas 1-6 (DD/PROOF.md lines 43-158). The source states these arithmetic assumptions (DD/PROOF.md lines 162-167, 268-271): correct rounding of the decimal module's exp, ln and sqrt, widened by one ulp (sqrt re-checked by squaring); the Gauss–Legendre error formula (Abramowitz–Stegun 25.4.30); Machin's formula with the alternating-series remainder for π; the erf series with alternating remainder and the stated erfc bounds; Stirling's formula at x + 40 with the first-omitted-term bound for Γ; and the incomplete-Gamma bounds (lines 130, 153). The corollary imports three inputs as decimal literals (dd_exact.py lines 17-18, 24-25): note CJ's J ∈ [4.1977819534, 4.1977820097], note DC's −Ĩ_quad ∈ [0.6104056, 0.6104057], and note V24's Theorem V allowances η_c, η_R for L ≥ 10 and L ≥ 24 in d = 2, 3. It computes 𝒮₂ = 25√3/(48π²) and 𝒮₃ = 125√30/(192π³) (lines 21-22; #242 Corollary 1′). The 38,288 node values are read from the stored node files, and the assembly recomputes 24 of them. Specific to certificate v1: ddvq._vcell forms each sliver cell from decimal endpoints with the centre rounded down and the half-width rounded up, and does not enlarge it. So at 2,694 narrow-regime nodes the node values omit sliver gaps (DD/PROOF.md lines 204-210). The source states that v1's printed numbers stand once control C10's Δ is added (item v1-c10-gap).",
+      "source_dependencies": "Inside the item: ddcore.py (30-digit directed-rounding decimal intervals, elementary functions, Taylor models); ddbounds.py (Lemma 1's monomial bound, the kernels and absolute Gaussian moments of Lemma 2, the Bell-polynomial ρ-derivatives and the Gauss constant K_n of Lemma 4, the cell remainder, the v-tail of Lemma 5); ddtaylor.py (T₁₁ and C₁₂ of Lemma 3 in exact Fractions); ddint.py (the integrand F of (1.3) and its t-moments); ddvq.py (the v-quadrature and H_point); ddgauss.py (rows, cells, Gauss nodes and weights, ω-tails of Lemma 6(ii), assemble); ddS.py (region S and the τ-tail, Lemma 6(i), (iii)); dd_exact.py (assembly and corollary); cells_full.json (62 rows, 2393 cells with stored remainders); nodes_0.json–nodes_11.json (38,288 node values). Sibling items of this object: v1-c10-gap (control C10, the sliver-gap omission) and v1-controls (controls C1-C9). Source note: frontiers/lifetime_dd_model_d_20261013/DD/PROOF.md (proposed QP-C54-021). Consumed sources and their records: #244 frontiers/soft_closed_form_20261002 (adopted Q-1119), #242 frontiers/soft_rejected_pairs_20261002 (adopted Q-1117), note V24 (proposed QP-C54-049), note DC (proposed QP-C54-048), note CJ (proposed QP-C54-020). Python standard library decimal, fractions and math modules.",
+      "source_self_limits": "DD/PROOF.md lines 268-271: nothing for d ≥ 4 or L < 10, no rate beyond notes C3 and C7, no improvement of one-sided bounds elsewhere, the enclosure is not sharp, and 'Theorem DD assumes the documented correct rounding of the decimal module's exp, ln and sqrt ... and the standard Gauss–Legendre error formula (Abramowitz–Stegun 25.4.30)'. Lines 8-9: 'Scientific effect NONE until read' and the #244 Theorem A scope. Remark 1 (lines 239-240): the Lemma 4 bounds exceed the true remainders by factors 10²–10⁶. README line 10 calls this directory 'certificate v1, the one the slice reads checked', and line 11 calls certificate v2 'the certificate of record'. The sliver-cell defect of v1 is at README line 27 and DD/PROOF.md lines 178-180, 204-210. README line 24: every read has organizational independence 0. Line 25: 'A PASS passes the slice as the note states it, conditional on the cited inputs, and discharges nothing'. Line 88: the finite controls 'do not prove the analytic statements'. The stored reads limit their own scope. REVIEW_SLICE_A.md line 1: 'This does not certify the numerical interval for `D` or Corollary DD1.' REVIEW_SLICE_B_AMEND.md line 30, not checked: 'The other 20 of `dd_exact.py`'s 24-node sample, and the 26–75 minute shard rerun'. REVIEW_C271.md line 3: 'not acceptance of the full numerical Theorem DD, Corollary DD1 or the project's finite-bar theorem'. REVIEW_C271.md line 183 excludes the interval primitives, the Taylor remainder and tensor/sliver quadrature implementation audit, and the 38,288-node replay.",
+      "finite_checks": "dd_exact.py makes the following checks with require, which is kept under -O. It requires a node record for every cell (line 37, a count of cell entries). It requires the cells file's Taylor parameters, Gauss order, row width, initial cells per row and row span to match the code (lines 40-42). It requires the 2393 cells to lie in the 62 rows, fill each row's rectangle exactly and be pairwise disjoint, in exact decimals (lines 43-58). It recomputes every cell remainder and requires equality with the stored value (C9a, lines 60-66). It recomputes 24 deterministically sampled node values and requires equality (C9b, lines 67-75). ddgauss.assemble (ddgauss.py lines 196-217) requires 16 records per cell with no 'FAIL' record and a finite lo ≤ hi at every node. It also requires the stored node-coordinate lower ends to equal the cell's node coordinates. That check runs only on records with at least six fields (line 212); every one of the 38,288 stored records has six, so it applies at every node. The recorded stdout of the assembly (results/assembly_stdout.txt, byte-identical to results/assembly_stdout_O.txt) reports C9a, C9b and the enclosures. The other 38,264 node values are taken from the stored files of hosted run 38347192184; the assembly does not recompute them.",
+      "floating_point": "USED in mesh, geometry and truncation-order choices; the bounds themselves are computed in decimal intervals. Every enclosure uses 30-digit decimal arithmetic with directed rounding (ddcore.py lines 10-17: context CD with ROUND_FLOOR, CU with ROUND_CEILING) and exact Fractions (ddtaylor.py; the rational constants in ddbounds.py). Python floats and math.* on floats appear in five places. (1) ddvq.py's v1_choice (lines 33-39); the source states any integer v₁ ≥ 3 is valid (DD/PROOF.md lines 181-182). (2) initial_cells' narrow-regime test, log-region parameters and cell edges. The edges are then rounded to the 10⁻⁶ grid by _short; the sliver endpoints come from the decimal exp enclosures of δ and ε without passing through floats. (3) ddgauss.py's g_hi_of and rows_for (lines 47-64). Their docstring calls g_hi a 'float heuristic; the tails are bounded rigorously whatever the choice', and the assembly reads these rows from cells_full.json. (4) ddint.py's choice of the series order K from float(Amax) and the float SERIES_TARGET (lines 19, 61-65); the remainder for that K is computed in decimal intervals (lines 86-93). (5) ddtaylor.py's Newton step count by math.log2 (line 56); the resulting root polynomial is required exact to degree N in Fractions (line 67). The other float conversions are display only (ddtaylor.py line 164, ddS.py line 139, ddgauss.py lines 101, 240). In certificate v1, _vcell does not enlarge a sliver cell, so a sliver cell can stop short of its right endpoint by up to about 10⁻²⁸ (README line 27); item v1-c10-gap encloses the omission. The numpy and float-cubature exploration values lie outside these files (DD/PROOF.md lines 228-230). Under A3 no float output counts as certification of the enclosure.",
